{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve the expression \\(\\frac{2}{1 + 2\\sqrt{3}} + \\frac{3}{2 - \\sqrt{3}}\\), we need to rationalize the denominators of each fraction. Let's start with \\(\\frac{2}{1 + 2\\sqrt{3}}\\).\n\n### Step 1: Rationalize \\(\\frac{2}{1 + 2\\sqrt{3}}\\)\n\nMultiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator, which is \\(1 - 2\\sqrt{3}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2}{1 + 2\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot \\frac{1 - 2\\sqrt{3}}{1 - 2\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{2(1 - 2\\sqrt{3})}{(1 + 2\\sqrt{3})(1 - 2\\sqrt{3})}\n\\]\n\nThe denominator simplifies using the difference of squares:\n\n\\[\n(1 + 2\\sqrt{3})(1 - 2\\sqrt{3}) = 1^2 - (2\\sqrt{3})^2 = 1 - 4 \\cdot 3 = 1 - 12 = -11\n\\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2(1 - 2\\sqrt{3})}{-11} = \\frac{2 - 4\\sqrt{3}}{-11} = -\\frac{2 - 4\\sqrt{3}}{11} = \\frac{4\\sqrt{3} - 2}{11}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Rationalize \\(\\frac{3}{2 - \\sqrt{3}}\\)\n\nMultiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator, which is \\(2 + \\sqrt{3}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3}{2 - \\sqrt{3}} \\cdot \\frac{2 + \\sqrt{3}}{2 + \\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{3(2 + \\sqrt{3})}{(2 - \\sqrt{3})(2 + \\sqrt{3})}\n\\]\n\n","prefix_end":1046,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":false,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,279,7493,1124,11520,37018,90,17,15170,16,488,220,17,59,26888,90,18,3417,488,1124,37018,90,18,15170,17,481,1124,26888,90,18,3417,59,701,582,1184,311,24438,551,279,61165,2973,315,1817,19419,13,6771,594,1191,448,1124,11520,37018,90,17,15170,16,488,220,17,59,26888,90,18,3417,59,3593,14374,14822,220,16,25,54525,551,1124,11520,37018,90,17,15170,16,488,220,17,59,26888,90,18,3417,59,692,95155,279,63533,323,279,46912,553,279,63280,349,315,279,46912,11,892,374,17767,16,481,220,17,59,26888,90,18,11035,7731,59,9640,59,37018,90,17,15170,16,488,220,17,59,26888,90,18,3417,1124,50853,1124,37018,90,16,481,220,17,59,26888,90,18,3417,90,16,481,220,